# Disease Background
Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 3 (SCA3) is an inherited neurodegenerative disorder caused by an expanded 
CAG repeat in the ATXN3 gene. The expansion produces an abnormally long polyglutamine (polyQ) region 
in the ataxin-3 protein. This altered protein can become prone to abnormal folding and aggregation, 
contributing to dysfunction and degeneration of neurons, particularly in the nervous system.

# Gene and Normal Protein Function
The ATXN3 gene provides instructions for producing the ataxin-3 protein, a deubiquitinating enzyme 
involved in cellular protein regulation and protein quality control. The normal ataxin-3 protein 
contains a polyglutamine region and helps regulate ubiquitinated proteins within cells. Changes in 
the number of CAG repeats in ATXN3 can alter the length of this glutamine-rich region and affect the 
properties of the resulting protein.

Reference transcript: NM_004993.6
Reference protein: NP_004984.2

# Documented Mutation
The documented mutation investigated in this study is a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in 
the ATXN3 coding sequence. For the computational model, the WT sequence containing 14 repeat units 
was expanded to 68 total CAG repeat units, adding 54 additional CAG repeats. Because each CAG contains 
three nucleotides, the expansion added 162 bp without changing the reading frame. Since CAG encodes glutamine (Q), 
the mutant protein contained 54 additional glutamine residues, increasing the predicted protein length from 361 to 415 amino acids.

# Methods
The ATXN3 WT coding sequence (NM_004993.6) was analyzed using Galaxy. The WT CDS was translated to obtain 
the predicted ataxin-3 protein sequence. A separate copy of the WT sequence was then modified by expanding 
the CAG repeat region to produce a 68-repeat model. The WT and mutant sequences were translated using Galaxy 
and compared based on CDS length, protein length, reading frame, amino-acid changes, and the presence of a 
premature stop codon.
A second experiment was performed by creating an artificial one-nucleotide deletion, removing the C nucleotide 
at c.426 (CDS position 396). The artificial mutant was translated and compared with the WT and documented mutant proteins.

# Results
The WT ATXN3 CDS was 1,086 bp and produced a predicted protein of 361 amino acids. The documented repeat-expanded sequence 
was 1,248 bp and produced a 415-amino-acid protein. The additional 162 bp represented 54 additional CAG repeats, resulting 
in 54 additional glutamine residues. The reading frame and normal terminal stop codon were retained.
The artificial c.426delC mutant had a CDS length of 1,085 bp. Because one nucleotide was deleted, the reading frame shifted 
from the deletion site onward, resulting in changes to many downstream amino acids and the appearance of premature stop codons.

