The WT and mutant proteins are identical before the expanded repeat region. The documented mutation adds 54 glutamine 
residues to the polyglutamine tract. Because the expansion consists of complete three-nucleotide CAG units, the reading 
frame is preserved and the downstream sequence remains aligned.

# Artificial Mutation Experiment
An artificial mutation was created by deleting one cytosine (C) at c.426 of the ATXN3 coding sequence, recorded 
as NM_004993.6:c.426delC. The deletion removed one nucleotide and therefore caused a frameshift. Translation of 
the artificial mutant showed changes in the downstream amino-acid sequence and the appearance of premature stop 
codons. This demonstrates how even a single-nucleotide deletion can substantially alter the resulting protein sequence.

Molecular Interpretation: Gene → Mutation → Protein → Cellular Effect → Phenotype

ATXN3 gene → CAG repeat expansion → expanded polyglutamine tract → altered ataxin-3 protein properties → abnormal 
protein folding/aggregation → neuronal dysfunction → neurodegeneration → SCA3

The ATXN3 gene normally produces ataxin-3. In the documented mutation model, expansion of the CAG repeat adds 
glutamine residues to the protein without shifting the reading frame. The resulting expanded polyglutamine tract 
can alter the properties of ataxin-3 and is associated with abnormal protein folding and aggregation. These changes 
can contribute to neuronal dysfunction and the neurological features of SCA3.

# Limitations
This study was limited to computational sequence analysis using Galaxy and did not include laboratory experiments 
to directly measure protein folding, aggregation, cellular function, or neuronal effects. The results therefore 
demonstrate predicted changes in DNA and protein sequences rather than directly proving the biological effects of 
the mutation. In addition, the documented mutation was modeled using a 68-repeat expanded allele, so it represents 
a computational model of a disease-associated repeat expansion rather than a single universal SCA3 mutation.

# Conclusion
The analysis demonstrated how a CAG repeat expansion in the ATXN3 gene can change the resulting ataxin-3 protein. 
The documented mutation added 54 glutamine residues while preserving the reading frame, whereas the artificial 
c.426delC mutation caused a frameshift and premature termination. Overall, the experiment showed how different types 
of DNA mutations can produce different protein-level consequences and helped illustrate the molecular relationship 
between ATXN3 mutations and SCA3.
